  imagePosition="75% center"
>
  <p>A cold bedroom does not always need to remain heated throughout the night.</p>
  <p>The actual problem may be a cold bed, a draught around the window, inadequate bedding or a room that loses warmth soon after the heater is switched off. Each problem points towards a different solution.</p>
  <p>An <a href="/guides/electric-blankets-vs-heated-throws/">electric blanket</a> can pre-warm the bed without heating the whole room. A <a href="/guides/reverse-cycle-heating-explained/">reverse-cycle air conditioner</a> can warm the room before bedtime and may provide controlled fixed heating where the climate or household genuinely requires it. <a href="/guides/fan-heater-vs-ceramic-heater/">Portable heaters</a> can provide short-term warmth while the room is occupied, but their quiet operation should not be treated as permission to leave them running while people sleep.</p>
  <p>Bedroom heating also needs to account for noise, airflow, controls, cords, children, pets and the distance between the appliance and bedding.</p>
  <p>This guide explains what to check before choosing a <a href="/guides/home-heating-options-australia/">bedroom heating option</a>.</p>
  <h2>Our Take</h2>
  <p>Separate bedtime comfort from overnight heating.</p>
  <p>Begin by deciding whether the household needs to:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>warm the bed</li>
    <li>warm one person</li>
    <li>warm the room before sleep</li>
    <li>maintain the room during unusually cold conditions</li>
    <li>address heat loss through the building</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These are not the same task.</p>
  <p>In many homes, a prepared room and warm bed will provide better overnight comfort than a portable heater left operating beside the bed.</p>
  <h2>Start with the bedroom itself</h2>
  <p>Before comparing appliances, inspect the room.</p>
  <p>Record:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>floor dimensions</li>
    <li>ceiling height</li>
    <li>external walls</li>
    <li>window size</li>
    <li>glazing type</li>
    <li><a href="/guides/bedroom-lighting-and-window-coverings-what-to-compare-before-you-buy/">curtains or blinds</a></li>
    <li>gaps around doors and windows</li>
    <li>flooring</li>
    <li><a href="/guides/home-insulation-basics/">ceiling insulation</a></li>
    <li>room orientation</li>
    <li>adjoining unheated spaces</li>
    <li>existing heating and cooling</li>
    <li>safe appliance positions</li>
    <li>wall power points</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Also consider how the room is used.</p>
  <p>A bedroom occupied only for sleeping has different requirements from:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>a nursery</li>
    <li>a bedroom used as a home office</li>
    <li>a room occupied by someone with limited mobility</li>
    <li>a guest room</li>
    <li>a child’s room</li>
    <li>a large bedroom with a sitting area</li>
    <li>a bedroom open to an ensuite or dressing room</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not size the heating requirement from the bed area alone when the room opens permanently into other spaces.</p>
  <h2>Bedroom heating options at a glance</h2>
  <p class="table-hint">Swipe sideways to compare the full table.</p>
  <div class="comparison-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Bedroom heating options at a glance comparison">
    <table class="comparison-table">
      <caption>Bedroom heating options at a glance</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Heating option</th><th scope="col">Usually suited to</th><th scope="col">Main advantage</th><th scope="col">Main limitation</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Electric blanket</td><td>Pre-warming a bed</td><td>Targets the sleeping surface rather than the whole room</td><td>Must fit correctly and be switched off before sleep</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Heated throw</td><td>An awake person sitting or reading</td><td>Localised warmth without heating the whole room</td><td>Not designed as an underblanket or routine sleeping product</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Reverse-cycle air conditioner</td><td>Regular room heating and summer cooling</td><td>Efficient fixed heating with thermostat control</td><td>Requires correct sizing, installation and acceptable airflow</td></tr>
        <tr><td><a href="/guides/oil-column-heater-vs-panel-heater/">Oil column heater</a></td><td>Quiet pre-bedtime room heating</td><td>Gradual warmth without a fan</td><td>Slow response, hot surfaces and portable-heater precautions</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Panel heater</td><td>Quiet background heating in an enclosed room</td><td>Slim profile and possible wall mounting</td><td>Can be obstructed easily and remains resistance heating</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fan or ceramic heater</td><td>Brief supervised warm-up</td><td>Fast heat</td><td>Fan noise and poor suitability for unattended bedtime use</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Radiant heater</td><td>Short direct warmth</td><td>Heat is felt quickly</td><td>Limited coverage and stricter placement concerns</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ducted heating</td><td>Bedrooms connected to a whole-home system</td><td>Central control and possible zoning</td><td>System design and minimum-airflow requirements matter</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <h2>Decide whether the bed or room needs warming</h2>
  <p>Heating the complete bedroom solely because the sheets feel cold may use more energy than necessary.</p>
  <p>Ask:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>Is the room uncomfortably cold while getting ready for bed?</li>
    <li>Is the main discomfort getting into a cold bed?</li>
    <li>Does the room remain comfortable once the occupant is under the bedding?</li>
    <li>Does cold air move across the bed?</li>
    <li>Does condensation form on the windows?</li>
    <li>Does the room become extremely cold overnight?</li>
    <li>Is continuous temperature management genuinely required?</li>
    <li>Is there an existing fixed system that has not been used effectively?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Where the bed is the main problem, electric bedding or more <a href="/guides/bedroom-essentials-for-a-more-comfortable-home/">suitable bedding</a> may be a better starting point than a larger room heater.</p>
  <p>Where the room itself is persistently cold, investigate heat loss, heater suitability and fixed-system options.</p>
  <h2>Electric blankets</h2>
  <p>An electric blanket commonly sits beneath the fitted sheet and warms the bed surface.</p>
  <p>It can suit bedrooms that do not otherwise need room heating throughout the evening.</p>
  <p>Before buying, check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>mattress size</li>
    <li>mattress depth</li>
    <li>fitted-skirt or tie-down design</li>
    <li>controller position</li>
    <li>one or two heating zones</li>
    <li>timer or automatic shut-off</li>
    <li>detachable controls</li>
    <li>cleaning instructions</li>
    <li>adjustable-bed compatibility</li>
    <li>storage requirements</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The blanket must be used in the configuration specified by its manufacturer.</p>
  <p>Do not assume an underblanket can be used over a person or that an electric overblanket can be placed beneath the sheet.</p>
  <p>Current Product Safety Australia guidance advises warming the bed and switching the electric blanket off before sleeping.</p>
  <h2>Inspect electric bedding each <a href="/seasonal/">winter</a></h2>
  <p>Before use, inspect:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>the fabric</li>
    <li>controller</li>
    <li>power cord</li>
    <li>plug</li>
    <li>connections</li>
    <li>heating area</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not use it if there are:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>frayed sections</li>
    <li>scorch marks</li>
    <li>damaged cords</li>
    <li>cracked controllers</li>
    <li>exposed elements</li>
    <li>unusual hard areas</li>
    <li>discolouration</li>
    <li>intermittent operation</li>
    <li>signs of pet damage</li>
    <li>a burning smell</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not attempt to repair internal wiring.</p>
  <p>Check the Product Safety recalls database where there is any concern about the model.</p>
  <p>At the end of the season, follow the manufacturer’s storage instructions. Current Australian product-safety guidance advises rolling electric blankets rather than folding them tightly.</p>
  <h2>Heated throws</h2>
  <p>A heated throw is designed to sit over an awake person on a chair or sofa.</p>
  <p>It can provide personal warmth while reading, watching television or working at a desk before bedtime.</p>
  <p>A heated throw is not normally designed to:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>sit beneath a sleeper</li>
    <li>replace an electric underblanket</li>
    <li>be tucked tightly around a mattress</li>
    <li>remain folded while operating</li>
    <li>operate beneath a person</li>
    <li>become a pet-heating mat</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Use it only as specified by the manufacturer.</p>
  <p>A heated throw can reduce the need to heat an entire room while one person is awake, but it does not prepare the mattress unless the instructions specifically permit that use.</p>
  <h2>Reverse-cycle air conditioning</h2>
  <p>A correctly sized reverse-cycle air conditioner can provide efficient room heating and summer cooling.</p>
  <p>It transfers heat from the outdoor air into the bedroom rather than producing all its heat through electrical resistance.</p>
  <p>For bedroom use, check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>heating capacity</li>
    <li>the relevant Zoned Energy Rating climate zone</li>
    <li>low-temperature performance where applicable</li>
    <li>indoor noise</li>
    <li>outdoor-unit noise</li>
    <li>indoor-unit position</li>
    <li>airflow over the bed</li>
    <li>lowest fan speed</li>
    <li>display brightness</li>
    <li>control sounds</li>
    <li>timer functions</li>
    <li>filter access</li>
    <li>defrost operation in colder regions</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A unit mounted directly opposite or above the bed may create uncomfortable airflow even when the room temperature is appropriate.</p>
  <p>The easiest wall for installation is not automatically the best position for sleeping comfort.</p>
  <h2>Using reverse-cycle heating before bed</h2>
  <p>A fixed reverse-cycle system may be useful for warming the bedroom before sleep.</p>
  <p>A practical routine may involve:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>closing the room</li>
    <li>closing curtains before outdoor temperatures fall</li>
    <li>setting a moderate target temperature</li>
    <li>allowing the room to warm</li>
    <li>directing airflow away from the bed where possible</li>
    <li>turning the system off at bedtime where suitable</li>
    <li>retaining warmth through bedding, insulation and draught control</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Australian Government winter guidance recommends turning heating off overnight and when the home is unoccupied.</p>
  <p>Some households may have different needs because of climate, age, health, building conditions or professional advice. Those circumstances require an appropriate fixed system and individual guidance rather than reliance on a portable heater beside the bed.</p>
  <h2>Sleep modes and timers</h2>
  <p>A sleep mode may adjust:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>temperature</li>
    <li>fan speed</li>
    <li>display brightness</li>
    <li>timer duration</li>
    <li>operating noise</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Its behaviour differs by model.</p>
  <p>Do not assume sleep mode means:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>silent operation</li>
    <li>no airflow</li>
    <li>the heater can be ignored</li>
    <li>the room will remain at one exact temperature</li>
    <li>the setting suits every sleeper</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>what temperature changes occur</li>
    <li>whether the fan continues running</li>
    <li>whether the display can be dimmed</li>
    <li>whether button beeps can be disabled</li>
    <li>what happens after a power interruption</li>
    <li>whether the unit restarts automatically</li>
    <li>whether the timer switches the complete system off</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A timer can support a planned routine. It does not correct poor placement, unsuitable sizing or excessive heat loss.</p>
  <h2>Defrost cycles in cold regions</h2>
  <p>During reverse-cycle heating, frost can form on the outdoor coil under suitable cold and damp conditions.</p>
  <p>The system may temporarily pause indoor heating to defrost the outdoor unit.</p>
  <p>During this period:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>the indoor fan may slow or stop</li>
    <li>room heating may pause</li>
    <li>outdoor sounds may change</li>
    <li>water or vapour may appear outside</li>
  </ul>
  <p>This can be normal.</p>
  <p>In a bedroom, a defrost cycle may be noticeable because the room is quiet and heating is interrupted.</p>
  <p>Professional assessment may be needed if:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>the outdoor unit remains heavily iced</li>
    <li>heating fails to resume</li>
    <li>defrost cycles are unusually frequent</li>
    <li>the room repeatedly loses comfort</li>
    <li>error codes appear</li>
    <li>abnormal sounds occur</li>
    <li>water freezes around paths or doors</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not chip ice from the outdoor unit or pour hot water onto it.</p>
  <h2>Oil column heaters</h2>
  <p>Oil column heaters provide gradual radiant and convective warmth without fan noise.</p>
  <p>They may suit supervised pre-bedtime heating in an enclosed bedroom where a safe floor position exists.</p>
  <p>Their disadvantages include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>slow initial warm-up</li>
    <li>hot metal surfaces</li>
    <li>a bulky footprint</li>
    <li>weight</li>
    <li>cord and castor considerations</li>
    <li>ongoing portable-heater precautions</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The sealed oil is not burned and does not normally need replacement.</p>
  <p>The heater retains some warmth after its element cycles off, but that stored heat came from electricity already used.</p>
  <p>Quiet operation does not make it suitable for unattended overnight use.</p>
  <h2>Panel heaters</h2>
  <p>Panel heaters provide quiet electric resistance heating, generally through natural convection.</p>
  <p>They may suit a bedroom where:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>a slim profile is useful</li>
    <li>wall mounting is permitted</li>
    <li>floor space is limited</li>
    <li>the room is enclosed</li>
    <li>a safe, unobstructed wall position exists</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Before choosing one, check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>wall material</li>
    <li>mounting requirements</li>
    <li>clearances</li>
    <li>curtains</li>
    <li>furniture</li>
    <li>lower air inlet</li>
    <li>upper warm-air outlet</li>
    <li>power-point position</li>
    <li>thermostat controls</li>
    <li>landlord approval</li>
    <li>whether hard-wiring is required</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not place a panel heater behind a bedhead, desk, chair or curtain.</p>
  <p>Wall mounting does not remove fire, heat or electrical risks.</p>
  <h2>Fan and ceramic heaters</h2>
  <p>Fan-assisted heaters provide rapid warmth.</p>
  <p>They may be useful while:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>getting dressed</li>
    <li><a href="/guides/preparing-your-home-for-winter/">preparing the room</a></li>
    <li>using a bedroom temporarily as an office</li>
    <li>occupying the room for a short supervised period</li>
  </ul>
  <p>They are generally less convincing for sleep because of:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>fan noise</li>
    <li>concentrated airflow</li>
    <li>short heat retention</li>
    <li>portable-heater placement</li>
    <li>bedding clearances</li>
    <li>the temptation to leave them operating unattended</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A ceramic heater is still generally a fan-assisted electric resistance heater.</p>
  <p>The word “ceramic” does not automatically mean:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>cheaper operation</li>
    <li>silent operation</li>
    <li>suitability for sleep</li>
    <li>lower electrical load</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Check the rated power, thermostat, noise and intended use.</p>
  <h2>Radiant heaters</h2>
  <p>Radiant heaters warm people and nearby objects directly.</p>
  <p>They can feel warm quickly, but the effect is strongest within the direct radiant zone.</p>
  <p>In bedrooms, they can introduce additional concerns around:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>exposed or hot heating surfaces</li>
    <li>bedding</li>
    <li>curtains</li>
    <li>clothing</li>
    <li>furniture</li>
    <li>children</li>
    <li>pets</li>
    <li>localised heat</li>
  </ul>
  <p>They are generally poorly suited to unattended bedtime heating.</p>
  <p>Do not point a radiant heater towards bedding or use it to dry clothing.</p>
  <h2>Ducted heating</h2>
  <p>A ducted reverse-cycle or gas heating system may already serve the bedroom.</p>
  <p>Before assuming the room needs another heater, check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>whether the bedroom zone is open</li>
    <li>supply-airflow strength</li>
    <li>return-air path</li>
    <li>door undercut or transfer air</li>
    <li>thermostat location</li>
    <li>zone schedule</li>
    <li>blocked grilles</li>
    <li>duct damage</li>
    <li>minimum open-zone requirements</li>
    <li>whether the system is designed to operate with the bedroom door closed</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not close every unused zone without understanding the system’s required airflow.</p>
  <p>A bedroom that remains cold despite an operating ducted system may have a balancing, duct, insulation or control problem.</p>
  <h2>Gas and combustion heaters</h2>
  <p>Do not use outdoor gas heaters, barbecues, charcoal appliances or other outdoor combustion products in a bedroom.</p>
  <p>They can produce carbon monoxide, which cannot be seen, tasted or smelled.</p>
  <p>Unflued gas heating introduces combustion products and moisture into the room and requires appropriate ventilation.</p>
  <p>A bedroom-heating plan should not involve blocking vents to retain heat around a combustion appliance.</p>
  <p>Existing fixed gas heaters should be:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>installed correctly</li>
    <li>maintained</li>
    <li>serviced by appropriately licensed people</li>
    <li>used with required ventilation</li>
    <li>operated according to the manufacturer’s instructions</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not sleep in a room with an unflued gas heater operating.</p>
  <h2>Noise</h2>
  <p>Bedroom noise can come from:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>heater fans</li>
    <li>compressor operation</li>
    <li>outdoor units</li>
    <li>thermostat clicks</li>
    <li>panel expansion</li>
    <li>oil column expansion</li>
    <li>moving louvres</li>
    <li>remote-control receivers</li>
    <li>timer relays</li>
    <li>control beeps</li>
    <li>vibration against walls or floors</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A showroom or daytime demonstration may not reveal what is noticeable at night.</p>
  <p>Check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>published indoor sound information</li>
    <li>the lowest fan speed</li>
    <li>thermostat cycling sounds</li>
    <li>whether control beeps can be disabled</li>
    <li>display brightness</li>
    <li>outdoor-unit position</li>
    <li>mounting vibration</li>
    <li>whether furniture amplifies sound</li>
  </ul>
  <p>“Quiet” is subjective unless test conditions are provided.</p>
  <h2>Airflow over the bed</h2>
  <p>Warm air blowing directly onto the bed can become uncomfortable.</p>
  <p>Before installing a fixed system, map:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>the bed position</li>
    <li>possible future bed positions</li>
    <li>wardrobes</li>
    <li>doors</li>
    <li>curtains</li>
    <li><a href="/guides/ceiling-fans-before-you-buy/">ceiling fan</a></li>
    <li>louvre movement</li>
    <li>unit servicing access</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A bedroom layout may change after installation.</p>
  <p>Check whether the indoor unit can:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>direct air down into the room</li>
    <li>swing away from the bed</li>
    <li>operate at a useful low fan speed</li>
    <li>maintain comfort without a concentrated draught</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not place a tall wardrobe or curtain where it can obstruct the indoor unit later.</p>
  <h2>Thermostat location</h2>
  <p>A thermostat or sensor does not measure every part of the bedroom equally.</p>
  <p>The sensor may be:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>high inside the indoor unit</li>
    <li>in a wall controller</li>
    <li>in the remote</li>
    <li>in a central part of a ducted system</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Warm air can collect near the ceiling while the bed level remains cooler.</p>
  <p>Check whether:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>the unit cycles off before the occupied level feels comfortable</li>
    <li>a remote-sensing function is available</li>
    <li>direct sun affects the sensor</li>
    <li>the bedroom door changes room pressure</li>
    <li>high ceilings cause stratification</li>
    <li>a ceiling fan’s approved winter mode could improve mixing</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not relocate or cover sensors without following product instructions.</p>
  <h2>Heating temperature</h2>
  <p>A higher thermostat setting does not necessarily warm the bedroom faster.</p>
  <p>It usually causes the heater to continue operating until a higher temperature is reached.</p>
  <p>Australian Government winter guidance suggests setting heating between 18°C and 20°C and notes that each additional degree can increase energy use.</p>
  <p>The appropriate comfort setting still depends on:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>the occupants</li>
    <li>bedding</li>
    <li>room humidity</li>
    <li>airflow</li>
    <li>building condition</li>
    <li>climate</li>
    <li>professional health advice where relevant</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Avoid overheating the room simply because the bedding initially feels cold.</p>
  <h2>Perceived dryness</h2>
  <p>Heating indoor air can lower its relative humidity even when the heater does not physically remove moisture.</p>
  <p>This may contribute to a sensation of dryness.</p>
  <p>Reverse-cycle heating mode and electric resistance heaters do not operate like a cooling-mode dehumidifier.</p>
  <p>Before adding a humidifier, check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>actual indoor humidity</li>
    <li>window condensation</li>
    <li>bathroom ventilation</li>
    <li>clothes drying</li>
    <li>leaks</li>
    <li>mould</li>
    <li>manufacturer cleaning requirements</li>
    <li>whether added moisture could worsen the room</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A humidifier can create another maintenance and moisture problem when used without measurement or cleaning.</p>
  <h2>Condensation and mould</h2>
  <p>A warmer bedroom can still develop condensation where moist indoor air meets cold glass or external walls.</p>
  <p>Possible contributors include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>inadequate exhaust ventilation elsewhere</li>
    <li>clothes drying indoors</li>
    <li>closed doors</li>
    <li>cold windows</li>
    <li>missing insulation</li>
    <li>furniture against external walls</li>
    <li>water leaks</li>
    <li>high indoor humidity</li>
    <li>uncontrolled air leakage</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Heating alone does not repair:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>roof leaks</li>
    <li>plumbing leaks</li>
    <li>failed window seals</li>
    <li>wet building materials</li>
    <li>inadequate bathroom extraction</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Persistent mould, dampness or water entry should be investigated rather than covered with fragrance or paint.</p>
  <h2>Curtains, blinds and windows</h2>
  <p>Windows can be a major source of bedroom heat loss.</p>
  <p>Check whether existing coverings:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>cover the complete glass area</li>
    <li>close without large side gaps</li>
    <li>reach the sill or floor where intended</li>
    <li>sit close to the wall</li>
    <li>use a pelmet or enclosed upper treatment where suitable</li>
    <li>remain outside heater clearances</li>
    <li>open easily for beneficial winter sun</li>
    <li>allow safe window operation and emergency egress</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Open suitable coverings when direct winter sun can warm the room.</p>
  <p>Close them before the outdoor temperature falls.</p>
  <p>Do not place portable heaters where curtains can move into the clearance zone.</p>
  <h2>Draughts</h2>
  <p>Common bedroom draught locations include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>beneath the bedroom door</li>
    <li>around external doors</li>
    <li>sliding-window brush seals</li>
    <li>loose window sashes</li>
    <li>ceiling access hatches</li>
    <li>skirting boards</li>
    <li>floorboards</li>
    <li>ensuite exhaust fans</li>
    <li>evaporative cooling outlets</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not seal every opening automatically.</p>
  <p>Check whether the gap is required for:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>ducted return air</li>
    <li>exhaust replacement air</li>
    <li>a combustion appliance</li>
    <li>ventilation</li>
    <li>window drainage</li>
    <li>subfloor moisture management</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Reduce uncontrolled leakage without preventing the room or building from ventilating safely.</p>
  <h2>Insulation</h2>
  <p>A bedroom can cool rapidly after heating stops when insulation is missing, damaged or poorly installed.</p>
  <p>Possible areas include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>ceiling</li>
    <li>external walls</li>
    <li>suspended floor</li>
    <li>raked roof</li>
    <li>wall adjoining a garage</li>
    <li>ceiling beneath an unconditioned roof space</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not enter a roof space casually to inspect insulation.</p>
  <p>Roof spaces can contain live wiring, fragile ceiling surfaces, dust, fibres and restricted access.</p>
  <p>Use existing records or arrange suitable professional assessment where required.</p>
  <p>A larger heater may mask the problem while increasing operating time.</p>
  <h2>Bedding</h2>
  <p>Appropriate bedding can reduce the need for room heating.</p>
  <p>Check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>seasonal quilt weight</li>
    <li>layered blankets</li>
    <li>fitted sheets</li>
    <li>mattress protector</li>
    <li>breathable materials</li>
    <li>whether bedding has become damp</li>
    <li>whether the sleeper becomes too hot after initially feeling cold</li>
    <li>laundering and drying instructions</li>
    <li>safe storage</li>
  </ul>
  <p>More layers are not automatically suitable for every person.</p>
  <p>Children, older people and people with health or mobility concerns may have different temperature-management needs. Seek appropriate professional advice where required.</p>
  <p>Do not place heavy items on an operating electric blanket.</p>
  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>Children may:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>touch heaters</li>
    <li>alter controls</li>
    <li>pull cords</li>
    <li>push bedding into clearance zones</li>
    <li>climb on portable appliances</li>
    <li>cover grilles with toys or clothing</li>
    <li>become too warm without recognising it</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>product age restrictions</li>
    <li>supervision requirements</li>
    <li>heater position</li>
    <li>cord access</li>
    <li>control locks</li>
    <li>surface temperatures</li>
    <li>furniture layout</li>
    <li>bunk-bed clearance</li>
    <li>smoke alarms</li>
    <li>clear exits</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A child lock only prevents control changes. It does not make the heater physically inaccessible.</p>
  <p>Portable heaters should not operate unattended in a sleeping child’s room.</p>
  <h2>Bunk beds and raised beds</h2>
  <p>Bunk and loft beds change the safe use of ceiling fans, heaters and cords.</p>
  <p>Check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>the distance to ceiling-fan blades</li>
    <li>access to wall-mounted heaters</li>
    <li>curtain movement</li>
    <li>climbing routes</li>
    <li>heater cords</li>
    <li>bedside electrical products</li>
    <li>whether bedding can fall onto an appliance</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not position a portable heater where bedding from an upper bunk can fall into its clearance area.</p>
  <p>Do not use a ceiling fan where safe clearance from the upper bunk cannot be maintained.</p>
  <h2>Older people and reduced mobility</h2>
  <p>A person with limited mobility may have difficulty:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>moving away from excessive heat</li>
    <li>reaching controls</li>
    <li>noticing a damaged cord</li>
    <li>responding to alarms</li>
    <li>moving a portable heater</li>
    <li>maintaining safe clearances</li>
    <li>leaving the room quickly</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A fixed, correctly installed system with accessible controls may be more appropriate than a portable appliance.</p>
  <p>People with reduced heat sensitivity or medical temperature-management needs should seek appropriate professional advice rather than relying on a generic buying guide.</p>
  <h2>Pets</h2>
  <p>Pets may:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>chew cords</li>
    <li>lie too close to heaters</li>
    <li>knock portable heaters</li>
    <li>push bedding into clearances</li>
    <li>scratch electric bedding</li>
    <li>block panel-heater outlets</li>
    <li>damage controllers</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not use a household heated throw or electric blanket as a pet-heating mat unless the product is specifically designed and approved for that purpose.</p>
  <p>Inspect powered bedding for claw, chewing or kneading damage.</p>
  <p>Keep pet beds outside heater-clearance zones.</p>
  <h2>Power points and cords</h2>
  <p>Bedroom heaters and electric bedding require safe electrical arrangements.</p>
  <p>Before buying, identify whether the product can reach a suitable wall power point without:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>crossing a walkway</li>
    <li>running beneath a rug</li>
    <li>passing through a doorway</li>
    <li>being trapped by a bed base</li>
    <li>stretching tightly</li>
    <li>using a double adaptor</li>
    <li>overloading a power board</li>
    <li>joining extension leads</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Current Product Safety guidance recommends one appliance per power point.</p>
  <p>Portable heaters should plug directly into a suitable wall outlet.</p>
  <p>Stop using any product where the plug, outlet or cord shows:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>cracking</li>
    <li>exposed wiring</li>
    <li>scorch marks</li>
    <li>looseness</li>
    <li>unusual heat</li>
    <li>sparking</li>
    <li>intermittent power</li>
    <li>a burning smell</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Damaged fixed electrical equipment must be assessed by a licensed electrician.</p>
  <h2>Heater clearance</h2>
  <p>Keep combustible materials at least one metre from portable heaters, including:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>bedding</li>
    <li>pillows</li>
    <li>curtains</li>
    <li>clothing</li>
    <li>throws</li>
    <li>furniture</li>
    <li>paper</li>
    <li>drying racks</li>
    <li>towels</li>
    <li>pet bedding</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The manufacturer may specify greater or additional clearances.</p>
  <p>Do not reduce the clearance because:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>the heater has a thermostat</li>
    <li>the surface feels cooler</li>
    <li>the unit is quiet</li>
    <li>it includes tip-over protection</li>
    <li>it is wall-mounted</li>
    <li>it has an economy setting</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Safety features are backups, not replacements for correct positioning.</p>
  <h2>Smoke alarms and escape paths</h2>
  <p>Bedroom heating should be considered alongside fire safety.</p>
  <p>Check smoke alarms according to:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>manufacturer instructions</li>
    <li>state or territory requirements</li>
    <li>rental obligations</li>
    <li>strata requirements</li>
    <li>local fire-service guidance</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Keep:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>bedroom exits clear</li>
    <li>keys accessible</li>
    <li>hallways free from heater cords</li>
    <li>furniture away from doors</li>
    <li>household escape plans current</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not position a heater where it narrows the route from the bed to the door.</p>
  <h2>Renters</h2>
  <p>Renters can improve bedroom comfort through reversible measures such as:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>using existing curtains effectively</li>
    <li>removable door snakes</li>
    <li>suitable rugs outside heater clearances</li>
    <li>safe electric bedding</li>
    <li>reporting failed window seals</li>
    <li>reporting damaged supplied heating</li>
    <li>closing unused rooms</li>
    <li>asking for permission before fixed installation</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Obtain written approval before:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>wall-mounting a panel heater</li>
    <li>installing a split system</li>
    <li>drilling into walls</li>
    <li>changing fixed electrical fittings</li>
    <li>replacing windows or permanent seals</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not conceal cords inside walls or make unauthorised electrical changes.</p>
  <h2>Apartments and strata properties</h2>
  <p>Fixed reverse-cycle installation may involve:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>external appearance</li>
    <li>common property</li>
    <li>balcony positions</li>
    <li>condensate drainage</li>
    <li>neighbouring bedrooms</li>
    <li>noise</li>
    <li>wall penetrations</li>
    <li>fire-rated construction</li>
    <li>electrical supply</li>
    <li>owners corporation approval</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A physical installation position may not be an approved position.</p>
  <p>Confirm responsibilities and written approvals before ordering the system.</p>
  <h2>Regional considerations</h2>
  <h3>Cool-temperate and alpine regions</h3>
  <p>Bedrooms may require more regular heating preparation.</p>
  <p>Priorities may include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>insulation</li>
    <li>curtains</li>
    <li>draught control</li>
    <li>fixed reverse-cycle performance</li>
    <li>defrost behaviour</li>
    <li>avoiding reliance on portable heaters during sleep</li>
    <li>suitable bedding</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Temperate coastal regions</h3>
  <p>Room pre-heating, electric bedding and moderate reverse-cycle heating may be sufficient in many homes.</p>
  <p>Check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>window heat loss</li>
    <li>outdoor-unit noise</li>
    <li>condensation</li>
    <li>coastal corrosion</li>
    <li>intermittent versus regular use</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Hot-arid regions</h3>
  <p>Warm days may be followed by cold nights.</p>
  <p>A short room warm-up and suitable bedding may be more practical than continuous heating.</p>
  <p>Use window coverings to retain useful daytime warmth where appropriate.</p>
  <h3>Tropical and subtropical regions</h3>
  <p>Heating may be needed only on occasional cool mornings or evenings.</p>
  <p>The heating mode of an existing reverse-cycle system, electric bedding or a short supervised portable-heater session may be sufficient.</p>
  <p>Do not compromise the room’s ventilation and summer performance for brief winter conditions.</p>
  <h2>Plan before the coldest part of winter</h2>
  <p>Where fixed installation is required, begin before the room becomes urgently uncomfortable.</p>
  <p>Planning allows time to:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>measure the room</li>
    <li>compare system types</li>
    <li>assess indoor-unit position</li>
    <li>assess outdoor-unit noise</li>
    <li>obtain written quotes</li>
    <li>confirm electrical work</li>
    <li>seek landlord or strata approval</li>
    <li>check installer licensing</li>
    <li>order equipment</li>
    <li>schedule installation</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Installer availability may tighten during cold spells.</p>
  <p>Reverse-cycle installers may also be heavily booked approaching summer, and some businesses operate with reduced capacity over Christmas and New Year.</p>
  <p>Planning does not guarantee a lower price or immediate installation. It provides more time to avoid an unsuitable decision.</p>
  <h2>When electric bedding may be enough</h2>
  <p>Electric bedding may be sufficient where:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>the bedroom is used mainly for sleeping</li>
    <li>the room is not dangerously cold</li>
    <li>the main discomfort is the cold mattress</li>
    <li>the bed can be warmed before sleep</li>
    <li>the product can be used safely</li>
    <li>the occupant does not need ongoing room-temperature management</li>
    <li>dampness and condensation are not being ignored</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>When fixed room heating may be more suitable</h2>
  <p>A fixed system may make more sense where:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>the bedroom is occupied for long periods</li>
    <li>the room doubles as a study or sitting area</li>
    <li>regular winter heating is required</li>
    <li>summer cooling is also needed</li>
    <li>no safe portable-heater position exists</li>
    <li>the local climate is consistently cold</li>
    <li>a suitable indoor and outdoor position is available</li>
    <li>airflow and noise can be managed</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>When the building needs attention first</h2>
  <p>Investigate the room before adding more heating where:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>the room cools immediately after heating stops</li>
    <li>curtains move with windows closed</li>
    <li>condensation is persistent</li>
    <li>mould keeps returning</li>
    <li>a leak is present</li>
    <li>walls or ceilings feel damp</li>
    <li>the window does not close correctly</li>
    <li>ceiling insulation is missing or disturbed</li>
    <li>large gaps are visible</li>
    <li>heating escapes into connected spaces</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The heater should not be expected to compensate indefinitely for a building defect.</p>
  <h2>Before You Buy Checklist</h2>
  <p>Before choosing a bedroom heating option, check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list" aria-label="Before you buy checklist">
    <li>Is the main problem a cold bed, cold person or cold room?</li>
    <li>Does the bedroom require heating throughout the night?</li>
    <li>Can the bedroom be warmed before bedtime instead?</li>
    <li>What are the floor dimensions?</li>
    <li>What is the ceiling height?</li>
    <li>Is the room open to an ensuite, robe or hallway?</li>
    <li>How much external wall and window area is present?</li>
    <li>Do curtains and blinds close properly?</li>
    <li>Are <a href="/guides/reduce-draughts-before-buying-bigger-heater/">draughts</a> present?</li>
    <li>Is ceiling, wall or floor insulation known?</li>
    <li>Does condensation or mould require investigation?</li>
    <li>Would improved bedding solve the immediate problem?</li>
    <li>Would an electric blanket suit the mattress and user?</li>
    <li>Can the electric blanket be switched off before sleep?</li>
    <li>Is electric bedding free from damage?</li>
    <li>Can controllers remain uncovered and accessible?</li>
    <li>Can cables avoid bed mechanisms and walkways?</li>
    <li>Is a heated throw intended only for awake use?</li>
    <li>Is a fixed reverse-cycle system practical?</li>
    <li>Has the heating side of the Zoned Energy Rating Label been checked?</li>
    <li>Does winter performance suit the local climate?</li>
    <li>Will the indoor unit blow directly onto the bed?</li>
    <li>Is the lowest fan speed acceptable?</li>
    <li>Are display lights and control beeps acceptable?</li>
    <li>Is defrost behaviour relevant to the climate?</li>
    <li>Is outdoor-unit noise acceptable near bedrooms?</li>
    <li>Is an oil column or panel heater being considered only for supervised room warm-up?</li>
    <li>Is fan-heater noise acceptable?</li>
    <li>Is rapid or gradual heat required?</li>
    <li>Is there a safe heater position?</li>
    <li>Can at least one metre be maintained from bedding and curtains?</li>
    <li>Can the heater connect directly to a suitable wall power point?</li>
    <li>Can one appliance be used per power point?</li>
    <li>Will cords remain clear of rugs, doors and walkways?</li>
    <li>Are children, pets or bunk beds present?</li>
    <li>Can heater controls be reached safely?</li>
    <li>Will the heater be switched off before sleep?</li>
    <li>Are smoke alarms maintained?</li>
    <li>Is the escape path clear?</li>
    <li>Are landlord or strata approvals required?</li>
    <li>Would a fixed system be more appropriate than portable overnight heating?</li>
    <li>Has installation been planned before peak demand?</li>
    <li>Has the proposed product been checked against the recalls database?</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Prepare the room and bed before adding more heat</h2>
  <p>Bedroom comfort is not determined by heater size alone.</p>
  <p>Begin with the bed, window coverings, draughts, insulation and the way the room is occupied.</p>
  <p>An electric blanket can pre-warm the bed and should be switched off before sleep. A reverse-cycle system can efficiently warm the room where fixed installation and regular use make sense. Oil column, panel, fan and ceramic heaters may provide supervised pre-bedtime warmth, but quiet operation and safety controls do not make portable heaters suitable for unattended sleeping use.</p>
  <p>Choose the solution for the problem the room actually has.</p>
  <p>A warm bed in a prepared room may provide better overnight comfort than a larger heater operating beside the bedding for hours.</p>

